
2026-09-19: A channel records who set each ban and when

A Channel used to keep nothing but the mask of a ban.  It now keeps, beside
the mask, who set the ban and when - Channel::getBanInfo() - and mod.debug's
CHANINFO reports both, one ban per line:

  Ban list (2):
    *!*@bad.host  set by alice  at 2026-09-19 17:43:47 (0 days, 00:00:00 ago)
    *!*@old.host  set by OldOp  at 2026-09-19 16:43:56 (0 days, 00:59:51 ago)

CHANINFO used to pack the masks into comma-separated lines, so a script that
reads its output needs adjusting.  Nothing else changes: the ban list itself,
what its iterators yield and xClient::OnChannelModeB() are as they were.

P10 carries neither a setter nor a time with a ban, so what is recorded is
what gnuworld saw, which is how ircu records a ban too: for a live MODE +b
the source's nick (a server's name for an OPMODE, a server MODE or a ban of
our own) and the time the line arrived; for a burst ban what the burst says,
since a P11 BURST sends each ban as a "mask timestamp setter" triplet, and
otherwise the bursting server and the time it arrived.  A ban gnuworld cannot
place a name on is shown as "set by (unknown)".

2026-09-19: ThreadWorker is the background-thread primitive

libgnuworld's gThread class is removed; ThreadWorker (threadworker.h) is the
background-thread primitive.  mod.scanner's dbThread, an empty subclass that
was never started, went with it.

2026-09-19: One hierarchical logger, in libgnuworld

GNUWorld's three loggers - the old elog stream, mod.cservice's own
structured Logger, and log4cplus for mod.dronescan - are down to one:
libgnuworld/logger.h, configured by an optional logging.conf (see
bin/logging.example.conf and doc/README.logger).  elog keeps working as a
deprecated compatibility shim; log4cplus and mod.dronescan are untouched.

A few things an existing install should know about before upgrading:

- debug.log is now appended across restarts instead of being truncated,
  and its lines use the new column layout documented in doc/README.logger.
- C++20 <format> is now a hard requirement at configure time (GCC >= 13, or
  Clang >= 17 with libc++); configure aborts without it.
- The IRC and console output of a log record now names the logger (the
  module) rather than the bot's nick; the nick is still available, as the
  JSON field "bot".
- A module other than cservice no longer creates an empty "<conf
  stem>.log" file just by starting; it logs nowhere until logging.conf, or
  its own code, gives it a sink.
- A log file this process creates is now mode 0640 rather than whatever the
  process's umask alone produced; an existing file keeps its mode.
- cservice's log_verbosity, chan_verbosity, console_verbosity, log_sql and
  console_sql are REMOVED: cservice is configured in logging.conf like
  every other logger, with the loggers "cservice", "cservice.commands" and
  "cservice.sql".  A cservice.conf that still has the five keys keeps
  working - they are not read, and cservice says so once at WARN when it
  starts.  Copy the (active) cservice section of bin/logging.example.conf
  into logging.conf to keep what those keys used to give: cservice.log, the
  debug channel and the console.  WITHOUT it, cservice's records go to the
  sinks of the root at the root's level, no cservice.log is written, and
  nothing of the log reaches the debug channel.  A SQL statement is now
  logged only when it fails, whatever log_sql said;
  "logger.cservice.sql = DEBUG" asks for every statement in full - never
  route that logger to an irc sink or a notifier.
- Pushover is a SINK TYPE of logging.conf ("sink.<id>.type = pushover")
  instead of something cservice attaches in code, so any logger can page -
  the root included, which is where the records most worth a page are (a
  FATAL of "core" or "core.net", another module's failure).  cservice's
  pushover_enable, pushover_token, pushover_userkey and pushover_verbosity
  are REMOVED; a cservice.conf that still has them keeps working and says
  so once at WARN, naming the keys and not their values.  What to put into
  logging.conf instead is in doc/README.logger's upgrade notes and in the
  commented-out section of bin/logging.example.conf.  Three things are new
  with it: the sink pages at ERROR and above unless logging.conf says
  otherwise (it was 3/WARN before), it sends at most 10 notifications a
  minute unless "sink.<id>.rate" says otherwise, and it never pages about a
  record of "core.notifier", which is where it logs its own delivery
  failures - so a pager on the root cannot feed itself.  logging.conf now
  holds a credential: chmod 600 it, which GNUWorld says once at WARN on
  "core.config" if the file is readable by anybody else.  A build without
  libcurl has no pushover sink and rejects a file that names one.
  Prometheus is unaffected: it is a metrics exporter cservice also calls
  directly, and it stays where it is.
- An irc sink can have a rate limit of its own, "sink.<id>.rate = 5/min",
  which it has not got unless the file asks for one: beyond it a record is
  dropped and counted, and the next one that does go out is preceded by a
  notice saying how many did not.
- LOGSQL_ERROR is now an alias of SQL_ERROR, usable in any module with a
  dbHandle, not only in one with a Logger of its own.
- cservice's command log no longer puts a command's arguments in the
  sentence a console or an IRC notice shows; they are in the JSON field
  command_line only.
- A number of elog lines that used to be printed unconditionally are now
  DEBUG or TRACE records, and so are quieter by default; raise the level of
  "legacy" in logging.conf to see them again.

See doc/README.logger for the full reference, the module author's guide,
migration notes from elog and from log4cplus, and how to build and test the
code behind the optional dependencies (test/docker/full-deps/run.sh).

The newest versions (v7.3.x) of postgresql do not come with c++ support by
default.  These libraries have been moved to a separate project.  However,
that project is not yet mature enough to compile no most systems.
Therefore, we recommend you use the latest version of pgsql v7.2.x
(currently 7.2.4).

